    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d818">

                <pb facs="1114" n="1104" xml:id="pg1104"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862.414/4–2845: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-04-28T13:00:00+02:00">April 28, 1945—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 5:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2176. From Murphy. With reference to my telegrams Nos. 2133, April 27, 8 p.m. and
                    2135, April 27,9 a.m.<anchor n="57" xml:id="d818-f1"/> two members of this
                    Mission and representative of British Element Control Council have made initial
                    inspection of between 200 and 400 tons German Foreign Office archives, half of
                    which discovered were in the Castle of Falkenstein, about 30 percent in the
                    Schloss Degenershaus and the remainder in the Burg Falkenstein and Schloss
                    Stolberg, all located in the Harz mountains near Geisburg. For security and to
                    facilitate adequate examination it is proposed to move the archives so [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">to</hi>] the University of Marburg in the American zone.
                    The bulk of the correspondence is pre-1943 and includes the original
                    anti-Comintern pact. The archives were in charge of Dr. Valentin and Baron von
                    Griesheim who voluntarily informed the American troop commander of whereabouts
                    of the archives. [Murphy.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
